Core Concepts Every DevOps Engineer Should Understand
In DevOps, technical depth goes beyond automation and deployment. These five concepts are essential for designing secure, scalable, and reliable systems:

✅HTTPS – Ensures encrypted communication over the internet. A solid grasp of SSL/TLS, certificate chains, and secure headers is essential for protecting data in transit.

✅SSO (Single Sign-On) – Simplifies user authentication across multiple platforms. Knowing how to work with OAuth, SAML, or OpenID Connect is crucial when integrating identity management in cloud-native systems.

✅SSH – Enables secure access to servers. Proper key management, access control, and session auditing are fundamental for infrastructure security and compliance.

✅SLA (Service Level Agreement) – Defines availability and performance expectations. DevOps engineers must align infrastructure design and monitoring with defined SLAs to meet business objectives.

✅CDM (Change Data Management) – Facilitates real-time data synchronization and change tracking. It plays a key role in version control, CI/CD pipelines, and maintaining consistency across distributed systems.

✅CDN (Content Delivery Network) – Distributes static and dynamic content across global edge locations to reduce latency and load times. Understanding caching strategies, invalidation, and edge security is essential for performance optimization.

✅IAM (Identity and Access Management) – Controls access to resources. Proper IAM policies reduce risk and ensure least-privilege access across environments.

These are not optional skills—they’re foundational to building secure, reliable, and scalable systems as a DevOps engineer.
